        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          DRYLOK and Flex Seal both get pulled up in the same "how do I stop this leak" searches, but they are not interchangeable. Pick based on the surface and the size of the problem, not brand recognition — using the wrong one wastes money and leaves the leak unsolved.
        </p>

        <p>
          It's an understandable mix-up. Both brands are heavily marketed as the definitive answer to water problems, and both have been around long enough to become household names — DRYLOK through decades of hardware-store shelf presence, Flex Seal through relentless infomercials and demo videos showing it patching a boat, a bucket, or a roof in seconds. But they solve different problems using different chemistry, and the comparison only makes sense once you know which category your leak falls into.
        </p>

        <h2 id="head-to-head" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          What Each Product Actually Is
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">DRYLOK</h3>
        <p>
          DRYLOK, made by UGL, is a cementitious and latex masonry waterproofing paint designed to be rolled or brushed onto poured concrete and concrete block. It works by penetrating into the porous surface of masonry and forming a continuous film that blocks water migration through the material itself. The flagship product, <strong>DRYLOK Extreme</strong>, is rated to withstand up to 15 psi of hydrostatic pressure — the pressure water exerts when soil around a foundation becomes saturated — which is why it's the standard recommendation for waterproofing basement walls from the inside.
        </p>
        <p>
          DRYLOK is not a spot-repair product. It's applied across an entire wall in two full coats, requires the surface to be bare, clean masonry (it won't bond over paint or grime), and is sold by the gallon and 5-gallon pail specifically because the jobs it's meant for are large.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Flex Seal</h3>
        <p>
          Flex Seal, made by Swift Response, is a rubberized coating built around liquid rubber and synthetic polymer chemistry. It's sold in several formats — spray cans, brush-on liquid, tape, and a thicker "Flex Seal Family" liquid rubber — and is marketed for sealing small leaks, cracks, and gaps across an unusually wide range of surfaces: metal, wood, PVC, aluminum, concrete, and fabric. The core selling point is flexibility: the cured coating stretches with the substrate instead of cracking, which is genuinely useful for something like a flexing gutter seam or a vibrating pipe joint.
        </p>
        <p>
          Flex Seal is explicitly positioned as a repair product for a defined leak or gap, not a whole-surface protective system. It's fast to apply and cures quickly, which is exactly why it works well for the emergency, small-scale jobs it's demoed doing.
        </p>

