        <h2 id="by-use-case-which-one" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          By Use Case: Which One Do You Actually Need?
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Basement or Foundation Walls</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Winner: DRYLOK.</strong> This is DRYLOK's core purpose. Damp, musty concrete block or poured walls need a coating engineered to penetrate masonry pores and hold back genuine hydrostatic pressure across a large surface. Flex Seal isn't formulated or tested for this scale of job, and using it to coat an entire basement wall would be both expensive per square foot and outside its intended use.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">A Single Leaking Foundation Crack</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Winner: Neither, technically — but Flex Seal is closer for a quick stopgap.</strong> The correct fix for an active leaking crack is a polyurethane injection kit, which fills the crack front-to-back and flexes with seasonal movement. Flex Seal can serve as a temporary surface patch on a small hairline crack while you source the right materials; DRYLOK is not designed to bridge an active crack on its own and will crack along with it if applied directly over an unfilled gap.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Gutters, Roof Flashing, and Metal Leaks</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Winner: Flex Seal</strong> — decisively. This is exactly the kind of job the brand is built for: a small, defined leak on metal or another non-masonry surface that benefits from a flexible, fast-curing rubberized coating. DRYLOK is a masonry-specific product and isn't formulated to bond with or protect metal roofing components.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Ponds, Planters, and Outdoor Containers</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Winner: Flex Seal</strong> for small containers and liners; DRYLOK for concrete ponds or masonry planters that need a permanent, pressure-rated coating. Flex Seal's flexibility and broad surface compatibility make it the practical pick for a cracked plastic or fiberglass container, while a masonry pond wall behaves more like a basement wall and benefits from DRYLOK's rigid, penetrating film.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Retaining Walls and Below-Grade Structures</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Winner: DRYLOK</strong> for masonry retaining walls; <strong>Flex Seal</strong> for a specific joint or penetration leak. A concrete or block retaining wall holding back saturated soil behaves exactly like a basement wall — it needs a coating rated for sustained pressure across a large surface, which is DRYLOK's specialty. Where Flex Seal earns a role is sealing a specific penetration, like a pipe sleeve or a control joint that's letting water through in one defined spot, rather than the whole wall.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">RV, Boat, and Trailer Roofs</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Winner: Flex Seal</strong> — this is squarely outside DRYLOK's product category. RV and trailer roofs are typically rubber, fiberglass, or metal, none of which DRYLOK is designed to bond with. Flex Seal's flexible rubberized formula is a genuinely common choice among RV owners for seam and vent leaks precisely because it moves with the roof material through temperature swings and vibration on the road.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Cost Per Job</h3>
        <p>
          DRYLOK runs roughly $45–$55 per gallon, covering about 75–100 square feet per coat — a typical basement wall job in the low hundreds of dollars for two coats. Flex Seal products range from about $10 for a spray can to $30–$40 for a gallon of liquid rubber, appropriate for the much smaller areas it's meant to cover. Comparing per-project cost only makes sense once you've matched the product to the job size; DRYLOK is not "more expensive" than Flex Seal, it's priced for an entirely different scale of coverage.
        </p>

        <h2 id="the-chemistry-behind-each-product" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          The Chemistry Behind Each Product
        </h2>
        <p>
          Understanding why these products behave so differently comes down to their underlying chemistry. DRYLOK is a cementitious-modified latex coating — it contains actual Portland cement particles suspended in a latex binder. When applied to porous masonry and allowed to cure, the cement component reacts with residual moisture and the calcium compounds in the concrete itself, effectively becoming part of the wall rather than just sitting on top of it. This is why surface prep matters so much: the chemical bond depends on direct contact with open, uncoated masonry pores. Paint, dirt, or a previous sealer physically block that reaction from happening, which is why DRYLOK peels so readily when applied over an unprepped surface.
        </p>
        <p>
          Flex Seal, by contrast, is built around liquid rubber polymer chemistry closely related to the rubberized undercoating used in automotive applications. It doesn't chemically bond with the substrate the way a cementitious coating does — instead it forms a continuous, elastic membrane that adheres mechanically to a huge range of surfaces through the polymer's natural tackiness and film-forming properties as its solvents evaporate. That's precisely why it works on metal, plastic, wood, and fabric where DRYLOK would fail: it isn't relying on a chemical reaction specific to masonry, just physical adhesion and elasticity. The trade-off is that a mechanically-adhered elastic film is inherently less suited to resisting sustained hydrostatic pressure across a large surface than a coating that becomes structurally integrated with the substrate.
        </p>

        <h2 id="application-walkthrough-what-each-job" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Application Walkthrough: What Each Job Actually Involves
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Applying DRYLOK to a Basement Wall</h3>
        <p>
          Strip any existing paint and scrub off efflorescence with a wire brush, then vacuum the wall thoroughly so the pores are fully open. Apply the first coat with a thick-nap roller or masonry brush using horizontal strokes, working the product into the surface rather than just laying it on top. Wait a full 24 hours, then apply the second coat in vertical strokes for complete, even coverage. Keep the area ventilated and avoid coating over any unfilled cracks — those need hydraulic cement or injection first. Budget a full weekend: a day for prep, a day spread across the two coats with the required cure time between them.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Applying Flex Seal to a Small Leak</h3>
        <p>
          Clean and dry the area around the leak — Flex Seal doesn't require the intensive prep DRYLOK does, but it still needs a surface free of loose debris, rust flaking, or standing water. For the spray formula, shake the can and apply in thin, even layers from about a foot away, letting each layer set for a few minutes before adding the next; two to three light coats generally outperform one heavy one. For the liquid or brush-on formula, apply directly with the included applicator or a disposable brush. Most Flex Seal products are touch-dry within a few hours and fully cured within 24–48 hours, which is why it's the go-to for a leak you need addressed the same day.
        </p>

        <h2 id="common-mistakes-with-each-product" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Common Mistakes With Each Product
        </h2>
        <p>
          The most frequent DRYLOK failure isn't the product underperforming — it's applying it over paint, dirt, or a wall that hasn't dried out from a recent repair. A close second is applying only one coat instead of two, which looks fine initially but doesn't deliver the rated pressure resistance the product is tested for. The third common mistake is coating directly over an active, unfilled crack and expecting the coating alone to stop the leak; DRYLOK is a surface-pore sealer, not a crack-bridging product on its own.
        </p>
        <p>
          With Flex Seal, the most common mistake is using it as a permanent fix for a problem that's actually structural — a rotted section of roof decking or a failed pipe fitting needs an actual repair, not just a rubberized coating over the symptom. The second common mistake is applying it too thick in one pass, which can leave the interior of the film uncured and tacky even after the surface looks dry. The third is expecting it to perform like a whole-surface waterproofing system when it was designed and tested as a spot-repair product.
        </p>

