          <h2 id="drylok-vs-flex-seal-who" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-white mt-0 mb-6 border-none">DRYLOK vs Flex Seal: Who Wins?</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6 text-gray-300">
            <p>
              <strong class="text-[#38BDF8]">Choose DRYLOK if:</strong> You're waterproofing a basement wall, foundation, or any large area of poured concrete or block masonry that experiences damp, musty seepage. You want a coating rated to hold back real hydrostatic pressure, and you're prepared to do the surface prep — bare, clean masonry — that makes it work.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong class="text-[#FF4500]">Choose Flex Seal if:</strong> You have a defined, small leak — a gutter seam, a roof flashing gap, a cracked pipe joint, a hole in a container or liner — on metal, plastic, wood, or fabric. You need something fast to apply that flexes with movement, and you're not trying to protect an entire wall.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg border-t border-gray-700 pt-6 mt-6">
              <strong class="text-white">The Honest Bottom Line:</strong> These products barely overlap in practice. DRYLOK is a masonry waterproofing system for whole walls; Flex Seal is a flexible patch-and-repair coating for small, specific leaks on almost any other surface. Match the product to the surface and the scale of the leak, and the "vs" question mostly answers itself. If you're still unsure which category your problem falls into, ask yourself one question: are you protecting an entire surface from ongoing exposure, or patching one specific spot that's already failing? The former points to DRYLOK; the latter points to Flex Seal.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is DRYLOK better than Flex Seal for a basement?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Yes, for basement walls DRYLOK is the correct product. It's a masonry waterproofing paint engineered to bond with and seal porous concrete, and it's rated to hold back real hydrostatic pressure. Flex Seal is a rubberized coating meant for small leaks and gaps — it isn't formulated or tested for whole-wall basement waterproofing.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use Flex Seal instead of DRYLOK?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Not for a full basement wall. Flex Seal can temporarily bridge a small crack or pinhole leak, but it isn't designed to coat and waterproof large areas of masonry against sustained water pressure the way DRYLOK is. For anything beyond a spot repair, use a purpose-built masonry waterproofer.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What is Flex Seal actually good for?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Small, specific leaks: a cracked gutter seam, a leaking pipe joint, a hole in roof flashing, a pond liner tear, or a rusted-through spot on metal. It flexes with the substrate and bonds to metal, plastic, wood, concrete, and fabric — a genuinely useful emergency-repair product, just not a substitute for a dedicated waterproofing system.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Does DRYLOK actually stop water completely?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">DRYLOK Extreme is rated to withstand up to 15 psi of hydrostatic pressure — enough for the dampness and seepage most basements experience through porous block or poured walls. It's not a fix for active flooding driven by a high water table; that requires drainage, not just a coating.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use DRYLOK on top of Flex Seal, or vice versa?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">It's not recommended. DRYLOK needs to bond directly with bare, clean masonry to work as designed, and a rubberized coating like Flex Seal underneath will block that bond. If you've already used Flex Seal as a spot patch, treat it as a separate repair and apply DRYLOK to the surrounding bare masonry instead.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does each product actually last?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">DRYLOK Extreme carries a 10-year warranty on properly prepped masonry and commonly performs well beyond that. Flex Seal's lifespan varies more by application — it holds up for years on a stable metal or plastic surface with limited movement or UV exposure, but a repair in a high-flex or high-sun area may need touch-ups sooner. Neither is a truly permanent, one-and-done fix in the way a structural repair is.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is one of these products safer to use than the other?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Both require basic precautions rather than special handling. DRYLOK is water-based and low-odor, though masonry dust from surface prep calls for a respirator. Flex Seal's spray formula releases aerosolized solvent and should be used in a ventilated area away from ignition sources, similar to any spray paint or rubberized coating. Read and follow the label on whichever product you're using, particularly around ventilation and cure time in enclosed spaces.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

